# Client video masters (.mov straight off the phone) are 30-120MB each and one
# exceeds GitHub's hard 100MB file limit, which blocks the push outright.
# Decision 6 Aug 2026: keep them out of git rather than take on Git LFS quota.
#
# The site is unaffected — it serves the transcoded public/videos/*.mp4, which
# ARE committed. These are archival originals only.
#
# Since 7 Aug 2026 they ARE backed up: scripts/sync-cdn.sh mirrors
# client-assets to the private, versioned S3 bucket shield-gate-repair-masters,
# which is the only copy of these files outside the working drive. Run it after
# adding footage. Masters committed before 6 Aug 2026 also remain retrievable
# from git history.
client-assets/videos/*.mov

# Local before/after SEO snapshots (scripts/seo-snapshot.ts)
.seo-snapshots/

# ── Client case-study source documents ──────────────────────────────────────
# The Word files and the zip they arrived in, 43 MB. Deliberately NOT committed:
# everything of value has already been extracted into the repo by
# scripts/extract-case-studies.py —
#
#   the 111 photographs  -> client-assets/case-studies/  (committed)
#   the web derivatives  -> public/images/case-studies/  (committed)
#   the write-ups        -> content/case-studies-client.ts
#
# So the .docx files are a redundant 43 MB of binary that git would store
# forever. If the extraction ever needs re-running, the originals are in the
# client's Drive export rather than here.
/case-studies/
#
# NOTE THE LEADING SLASH. Without it this pattern matches a directory of that
# name at ANY depth, which silently excluded client-assets/case-studies/ (the
# client's only copies of 111 job photographs) and public/images/case-studies/
# (596 web derivatives the pages reference). The site would have built and
# deployed with every case-study image 404ing.
